const faqItems = [
  { question: "Did the Maine adult-use cannabis excise tax move to 10% of wholesale price on January 1, 2026?", answer: "No. The excise tax stayed weight-based (per pound, per mature plant, per immature plant or seedling, per seed). What P.L. 2025, ch. 388 (LD 210) did was reduce the per-weight rates by approximately one-third effective January 1, 2026. The new rates under 36 M.R.S. §4923 are: cannabis flower $223/lb (down from $335/lb), cannabis trim $63/lb (down from $94/lb), immature plants/seedlings $1.00 each (down from $1.50), mature plants $23 each (down from $35), and cannabis seeds $0.20 each (down from $0.30). A 10% wholesale-price excise was proposed in earlier bill text (SP 559, 131st Legislature) but the enacted version kept the per-weight structure. The separate 14% figure that took effect January 1, 2026 is the adult-use retail sales tax, not the excise tax." },
  { question: "When does the new 2026 Maine cannabis excise tax take effect and what is the new rate?", answer: "The reduced per-weight excise tax rates under P.L. 2025, ch. 388, Pt. F, §3 took effect January 1, 2026. The amended 36 M.R.S. §4923 specifies: cannabis flower $223/lb, cannabis trim $63/lb, immature plants/seedlings $1.00 each, mature plants $23 each, and cannabis seeds $0.20 each. Each rate is a roughly one-third reduction from the prior 2019-2025 schedule. The tax is paid by the cultivation facility licensee on the sale or transfer of adult-use cannabis to other licensees. The Maine Revenue Services General Information Bulletin 115 (October 17, 2025) confirms the new rates." },
  { question: "What does LD 1654 (P.L. 2025, ch. 504) actually do for cannabis operators?", answer: "LD 1654 (P.L. 2025, ch. 504), effective July 29, 2026, adds two new excise tax exemptions to 36 M.R.S. §4923. First, sales or transfers of adult-use cannabis between cultivation facilities are exempt from the excise tax (new §4923(7)). Second, transfers of adult-use cannabis from a products manufacturing facility back to the original cultivator are exempt if the cannabis is returned in the same form and weight within 30 days. The 30-day window is a product-return eligibility period for the exemption, not a payment grace period. The originally-introduced 120-day payment grace period was removed by committee amendment before enactment. The actual text of the law is the transfer exemption described in the Maine Legislature's enacted-law summary (legislature.maine.gov/doc/12549)." },
  { question: "How much will the OCP Metrc contract price increase cost my cannabis business in 2026?", answer: "The OCP's new 5-year contract with Metrc took effect February 4, 2026. The price increases were negotiated with a delay to the May 1, 2026 effective date. The new prices are: monthly user fee $45 (up from $40, +12.5%); package tag $0.26 (up from $0.25, +4.0%); individual plant tag $0.46 (up from $0.45, +2.2%); 5-plant batch tag $2.30 (up from $2.25); 10-plant batch tag $4.60 (up from $4.50); 25-plant batch tag $11.50 (up from $11.25); 50-plant batch tag $23.00 (up from $22.50). For a mid-size cultivation facility running 5,000 plants per year on individual tags, the annual tag cost rises by $50; for a 50,000-plant operation, the rise is $500 per year. The monthly user fee change is the bigger number for most operators." },
  { question: "What is the new 14% adult-use cannabis tax in Maine?", answer: "The 14% is the adult-use retail sales tax under 36 M.R.S. §1811(1)(D)(5), effective January 1, 2026, enacted via P.L. 2025, ch. 388, Pt. F. It is collected by the retailer at the point of sale to the consumer, not by the cultivator on wholesale transfers. The 14% is an increase from the 10% retail sales tax in effect 2020-2025. The 5.5% standard state sales tax does not apply to adult-use cannabis (it is replaced by the 14% adult-use rate); the 8% rate on edibles as food products continues to apply." },
  { question: "Are there any Maine cannabis excise tax exemptions I should know about?", answer: "Yes, two important ones for the 2026 tax year. First, under amended 36 M.R.S. §4923(7) added by P.L. 2025, ch. 504 (LD 1654), sales or transfers of adult-use cannabis between cultivation facilities are exempt from the excise tax effective July 29, 2026. Second, transfers of adult-use cannabis from a products manufacturing facility back to the original cultivator are exempt if the cannabis is returned in the same form and weight within 30 days. Prior to LD 1654, inter-cultivator transfers were taxed at the standard per-weight rate. The 20% sales tax on hemp and hemp products containing THC imposed by P.L. 2025, ch. 388 (LD 210) is a separate retail-level tax collected at the register, not an excise exemption." },
  { question: "Did the 2026 budget change the transfer to the Adult Use Cannabis Public Health and Safety and Municipal Opt-in Fund?", answer: "Yes. P.L. 2025, ch. 388 (LD 210) reduced the percentage transferred from the adult-use cannabis sales tax and excise tax to the Adult Use Cannabis Public Health and Safety and Municipal Opt-in Fund from 12% to 6% effective January 1, 2026. This reduces the share of cannabis tax revenue directed to municipal opt-in reimbursements and public health programming, with the corresponding increase in revenue retained for the General Fund. The municipal opt-in reimbursement program ($20,000 per opted-in municipality within three years of opt-in) continues but the funding source is now the smaller 6% allocation." },
  { question: "Will the federal Schedule III reclassification affect Maine excise tax compliance?", answer: "No. The state excise tax is levied on the cultivation facility on the sale or transfer of adult-use cannabis, not on the federal scheduling of the product. The April 28, 2026 DOJ Final Order that moved state-licensed medical cannabis to Schedule III has no effect on the adult-use excise tax base, rate, or payment schedule. The state excise tax, the state retail sales tax, the municipal opt-in transfer, and the Metrc contract pricing are all state-level instruments, unchanged by the federal action. The Schedule III move affects federal income tax deductibility for the medical side of a dual-license operation, not the cultivation excise tax." }
];
